# PowerShell Commands on Windows

**Trigger**: Always active on Windows (`win32`). Prefer native PowerShell cmdlets over Unix-style aliases.

## Core Rule

When the OS is Windows, use PowerShell cmdlets instead of Unix commands. The `Shell` tool runs PowerShell by default on Windows — use its native syntax.

## Command Mapping

| Unix Command | PowerShell Cmdlet |
|---|---|
| `ls` | `Get-ChildItem` |
| `cat` | `Get-Content` |
| `grep` | `Select-String` (or use `rg` / `ripgrep`) |
| `find` | `Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter` |
| `cp` | `Copy-Item` |
| `mv` | `Move-Item` |
| `rm` | `Remove-Item` |
| `mkdir` | `New-Item -ItemType Directory` |
| `touch` | `New-Item -ItemType File` |
| `pwd` | `Get-Location` |
| `cd` | `Set-Location` |
| `echo` | `Write-Output` |
| `head -n 5` | `Get-Content -TotalCount 5` |
| `tail -n 5` | `Get-Content -Tail 5` |
| `wc -l` | `Measure-Object -Line` |
| `export VAR=val` | `$env:VAR = "val"` |
| `echo $VAR` | `Write-Output $env:VAR` |
| `sleep 5` | `Start-Sleep -Seconds 5` |
| `curl URL` | `Invoke-WebRequest -Uri URL` |
| `chmod +x file` | Not needed — Windows uses file extensions, not execute bits |
| `which cmd` | `Get-Command cmd` |
| `kill PID` | `Stop-Process -Id PID` |

## File Search Patterns

```powershell
# ❌ BAD — Unix find
find . -name "*.tsx" -type f

# ✅ GOOD — PowerShell
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter "*.tsx" -File

# ✅ ALSO GOOD — ripgrep (cross-platform, installed)
rg --files -g "*.tsx"
```

## File Content Search

```powershell
# ❌ BAD — Unix grep
grep -r "export.*function" src/

# ✅ GOOD — Select-String
Select-String -Path "src/**/*.ts" -Pattern "export.*function" -List

# ✅ ALSO GOOD — ripgrep (preferred for code search)
rg "export.*function" src/
```

## Directory Listing

```powershell
# ❌ BAD
ls -la

# ✅ GOOD
Get-ChildItem -Force | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending

# ✅ ALSO GOOD — just use ls alias if simple listing suffices
Get-ChildItem
```

## Multi-File Operations

```powershell
# ❌ BAD — Unix pipeline
find . -name "*.log" -exec rm {} \;

# ✅ GOOD — PowerShell pipeline
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter "*.log" | Remove-Item -Force
```

## Environment Variables

```powershell
# ❌ BAD — Unix export syntax
export DATABASE_URL="postgresql://localhost/db"
echo $DATABASE_URL

# ✅ GOOD — PowerShell environment variables
$env:DATABASE_URL = "postgresql://localhost/db"
Write-Output $env:DATABASE_URL

# ✅ Reading multiple env vars
Write-Output "$env:NODE_ENV | $env:PORT"
```

## When to Use Unix Commands Anyway

Use Unix-style commands only when:

1. **Cross-platform tools are installed**: `rg`, `fd`, `git`, `pnpm` — these work identically on Windows
2. **Chained shell commands**: Use `&&` in PowerShell for sequential execution (PowerShell 7+ supports it)
3. **Node/pnpm scripts**: Package.json scripts should remain cross-platform or use `pnpm dlx` wrappers
4. **Git commands**: `git status`, `git log`, `git add`, `git commit` work identically on Windows

## Path Handling

```powershell
# ❌ BAD — forward slashes in native cmdlets
Get-ChildItem src/components

# ✅ GOOD — PowerShell handles both, but be explicit with variables
$path = Join-Path "src" "components"
Get-ChildItem $path

# ✅ GOOD — using variable expansion
$srcDir = "src\components"  # backslash is native Windows
Get-ChildItem $srcDir
```

## Process Management

```powershell
# ❌ BAD — Unix process management
ps aux | grep node
kill -9 12345

# ✅ GOOD — PowerShell process management
Get-Process -Name node
Stop-Process -Name node -Force
```

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Correct Behavior |
|---|---|
| Using `cat file.txt` in PowerShell | Use `Get-Content file.txt` |
| Using `ls -la` for detailed listing | Use `Get-ChildItem -Force` |
| Using `grep pattern *.ts` | Use `Select-String` or `rg` |
| Using `find . -name "*.ts"` | Use `Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter "*.ts"` |
| Using `export VAR=val` | Use `$env:VAR = "val"` |
| Using `chmod +x script.sh` | Not needed on Windows; run `.ps1` directly |
| Using `curl URL` (Unix) | Use `Invoke-WebRequest -Uri URL` |

## Cross-Reference Requirement

Any rule or skill containing shell commands MUST:

1. **Link to this rule** — Add comment: "See `powershell-commands-windows.mdc` for Windows equivalents"
2. **Provide PowerShell examples** — Include PowerShell alongside Unix examples, or use cross-platform tools
3. **Use cross-platform tools when possible** — Prefer `pnpm`, `git`, `docker`, `rg` over native OS commands

Example reference in other rules:

```markdown
## Running Commands

See `powershell-commands-windows.mdc` for Windows equivalents.

```powershell
pnpm run build
```
```

This ensures Windows users have consistent guidance across all rules and skills.
